From 52e8e4f732ff2b61625616d8a0e2f97659540019 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 14:51:22 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] state: online FTS recovery frees healthy indexes and unlinks
 only damaged ones; review fixes [daveey]

Review of bc73674b: unlinking a HEALTHY index (the futile-holder case) left pages that every
whole-file check reads as damage: the Desktop update preflight refuses, doctor/repair escalate to
drop_fts_vacuum, browse can latch the store corrupt. And a scoped check cannot tell whether an
index shares pages with another structure, so freeing one is only safe after a whole-file check.

- plan_online_recovery: one read-only whole-file integrity_check. Damage outside the FTS families
  (or a check that cannot complete) refuses (`hermes sessions recover`). A family named by the
  check (Tree lines, FTS5 xIntegrity lines) is unlinked; every other family is trashed: vtable row
  deleted, shadow tables renamed fts_v22_trash_*, drained and dropped by the existing chunked
  teardown, so a healthy index ends with integrity_check == ok.
- advance_fts_backfill also runs the trash teardown; tick budget 5 s.
- _fts_oneshot_failed only on a corrupt-image error (a lock error no longer goes online).
- CLI --wait stops on no progress and never prints success it did not reach.
- Futile log line wording; docs: cli-commands, session-storage-recovery.

Tests: hub damage shape (a child page named twice: integrity_check completes with "2nd reference",
"out of order", "never used") with a resident holder; healthy futile case ends clean and passes
_db_opens_cleanly, verify_sqlite_integrity and the backup preflight; root-page damage that makes the
check fail is refused; damage in `sessions` is refused; lock vs corrupt one-shot failure; CLI.

Found while testing, not changed here: on the hub's damage shape the EXISTING one-shot rebuild's
DROP can succeed and free the shared page twice (a fresh corruption) when no other process holds
the store.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
